WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.801 --> 00:00:03.634
(bluegrass music)

00:01:04.187 --> 00:01:06.310
- [Mark] All right,
let me hear your voice.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.150
I wanna see how loud you are.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.370
- How loud are we?

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.120
- [Mark] Perfect, perfect, perfect.

00:01:12.597 --> 00:01:15.436
- I can get a little
louder if you want me to.

00:01:15.436 --> 00:01:16.269
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:01:16.269 --> 00:01:17.677
- No, I'm sure you will
with the right questions.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:21.600
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:22.630
All right, Mountain Man.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:23.463
- Yeah.

00:01:23.463 --> 00:01:24.296
- [Mark] Mountain Man,
where'd you grow up?

00:01:24.296 --> 00:01:25.640
Where are you from originally?

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.540
- Right here on Straight Creek, buddy.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.354
This the only place to grow
up, is on Straight Creek.

00:01:31.354 --> 00:01:32.607
- [Mark] Tell me about your family.

00:01:32.607 --> 00:01:34.590
You had both your parents growing up?

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:35.700
- Yeah, dad, and all that.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.130
Well, dad and them was
raised here, around here.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:42.780
And, we was up the road
there a little ways,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.480
was where dad went in when he got married,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:45.870
we was all raised up there.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:50.160
But this is grandma's and
dad's old house place, really.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.050
- [Mark] And how would you
describe your childhood?

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:53.883
- Beautiful.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.370
And we had all ways of fun

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.970
of running up and down this road here.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:04.543
But, if we went too far...
we went fishing one day,

00:02:05.511 --> 00:02:08.857
and dad said, "Don't go no
further in the water gap, boys."

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:14.030
We go down the road, we
go on by the water gap,

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.580
you know, we was catching fish.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.693
We go on by the water gap,

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.020
it's a gettin' about this
time, late in the evening,

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.670
gettin' dark.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:28.670
And, we looked up the road
and here come dad with a limb,

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:31.653
was swappin' it on his leg.

00:02:31.653 --> 00:02:33.190
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:36.180
Well, now we know, we figured
he wouldn't whip us, you know.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.810
He had a (indistinct)

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.640
Had a big string of our fish.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.970
He wore us out and
said, "Now boys, go home

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:44.843
and clean the fish and we'll eat 'em.

00:02:44.843 --> 00:02:45.844
(Mountian Man laughing)

00:02:45.844 --> 00:02:47.790
He said, "I told you not to go no further

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:48.790
than the water gap."

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:55.090
We figured we'd get by
without getting a whippin'

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:56.980
if we had that big stringer of fish.

00:02:58.703 --> 00:03:01.800
So, we, we, we got what they deserved

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.521
'cause we disobeyed him. You know.

00:03:03.521 --> 00:03:05.453
Didn't do what he said.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.060
And if it was, these old-timers,

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.330
if you didn't do what they
said, you got a whippin'.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.037
- [Mark] What is a whippin'?

00:03:15.037 --> 00:03:17.001
- A wearin' out with a limb.

00:03:17.001 --> 00:03:17.834
- [Mark] With a stick?

00:03:17.834 --> 00:03:20.050
And the most of the time,
you had to go cut it

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.913
and get your limb to be whipped with.

00:03:24.293 --> 00:03:27.520
We'd go get one of them
little clean winter limbs

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.215
so they'd break quick
and he'd quit whipping.

00:03:30.215 --> 00:03:32.247
(Mountian Man laughing)

00:03:32.247 --> 00:03:33.830
- [Mark] What do you, how
do you, how do you say it?

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:35.580
Appalachia or Appalachia?

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.520
- Appalachian or whatever.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:39.353
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:03:39.353 --> 00:03:41.807
And, it's a beautiful part of the country.

00:03:41.807 --> 00:03:46.480
- Yeah, it is. I, I
wouldn't live no where else.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:47.313
- [Mark] You're very poor though.

00:03:47.313 --> 00:03:48.410
- Yeah.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.140
- [Mark] Tell me, tell
me about how you grew up.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:51.920
You grew up with electricity,
and running water,

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:52.790
and stuff like that?

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:54.780
- Well, no, we didn't have no electricity,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.790
didn't have no electric
or running water either.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.720
We run it out of the,
got it out of the spring.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.210
But, they eventually got the
electricity up through here.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.823
We had a telephone, they got telephones.

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:11.440
Party line.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.897
Yeah, you know, "Hey,
let me use the phone."

00:04:13.897 --> 00:04:16.690
"Hey, get off the phone.
I gotta talk to somebody."

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:18.962
That's the only way you get the phone.

00:04:18.962 --> 00:04:23.920
And we was kids, and that
old cable, they run that,

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.767
and we'd climb up that pole up in a limb

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:27.386
and get ahold of that line

00:04:27.386 --> 00:04:28.907
and it would knock the
daylights out of ya.

00:04:28.907 --> 00:04:29.910
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.690
We thought we'd get up there, hold on it,

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.310
and see what was going on.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.753
We didn't hold on to it long

00:04:35.753 --> 00:04:37.347
'cause it was juice on that old,

00:04:37.347 --> 00:04:40.483
it was just a piece of wire, what it was.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.110
- [Mark] So, tell me
about how you grew up.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.350
You had transportation?

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:50.140
- Yeah. Well, we had, we
got one pair of shoes a year

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.004
and you better not wear 'em out

00:04:54.004 --> 00:04:55.670
'cause you wouldn't, you
wouldn't get no more.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.620
And we'd go barefoot in the summertime.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.083
We never went nowhere
unless we went barefooted.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.560
We'd go fishing down the old creek bank,.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:06.710
We had an old black dog

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.453
and he killed every snake in front of ya.

00:05:10.453 --> 00:05:13.160
He didn't matter what kind
come out, he'd kill that snake.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:14.560
We didn't have to worry about the snakes,

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.650
we had the dog with us.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:19.660
So, when you got down in here,

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.760
and then we got up here at Iva Taylor's

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:23.260
up here at this pond.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:26.423
They had a big strawberry patch.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:31.370
We'd go fishing, we went down,

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:34.580
we was gonna crawl over
in his strawberry patch,

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:36.183
pick us a bunch of strawberries.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.280
We go crawl through them weeds and stuff,

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:43.050
crawled up on an old copperhead.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.679
Hey, boy, that was the end
of the strawberry gettin'.

00:05:45.679 --> 00:05:46.580
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:48.590
We left town.

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:51.473
We had us a water hole up
here, went swimming in.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:57.473
Well, Keith Coldarn had an old collie dog,

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.090
and if you went swimming

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.910
and started squalling and a screaming,

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.660
that dog come in there,

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:06.650
and if you didn't get,
go to shore with him,

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.880
he'd drown ya tryin'
to get ya outta there.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.750
We'd get to the shore and
quit, wasn't squalling no more,

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:15.170
there was no hollerin' 'cause
the dog'd come in and get ya.

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:17.283
He thought you was a drownin', I'd say.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:19.856
- [Mark] Tell me about how you grew up.

00:06:19.856 --> 00:06:20.947
I assume you were poor?

00:06:20.947 --> 00:06:22.990
- No, we were, what we raised, we would,

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:25.363
what we raised, what we eat.

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:30.600
Every summer dad'd buy, we
traded the old peach man,

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.560
we dread him a coming round.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:34.763
He'd buy 13 bushel of peaches.

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:38.720
We had to peel them and can 'em.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.560
We put 300 cans of stuff in the cellar

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.380
and up at the old house.

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:47.140
They'd can 300 a year,
every, different stuff.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.250
All, everything was canned.

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:53.060
You buy nothin' at the
store except necessities,

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.490
like meal.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:58.203
We take the corn bread,
corn over in Wilhoite,

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:05.013
and have it ground. And
that way we got our meal.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.783
And dad raised honeybees,

00:07:09.082 --> 00:07:11.560
and most the time that's
what we used for chewing gum.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:15.221
The wax off it, you
know, well, it was good.

00:07:15.221 --> 00:07:16.054
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:07:16.054 --> 00:07:17.200
There wasn't no doubt about that.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:18.340
- [Mark] How much schooling did you get?

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:19.227
How far do you go in school?

00:07:19.227 --> 00:07:20.060
- Huh?

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.590
- [Mark] How far did you go in school?

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:23.703
- Went to a year of high school.

00:07:23.703 --> 00:07:25.444
- [Mark] And you went to work after that.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:26.277
- Huh?

00:07:26.277 --> 00:07:27.260
- [Mark] You went to work after that?

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:29.993
- Yeah. Well, I got, yeah,
I went to North Carolina.

00:07:31.702 --> 00:07:36.702
And down there, when I
turned 18, got drafted,

00:07:38.937 --> 00:07:40.420
you know, to serve.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:43.130
I went down there with my sister.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:46.270
But, now we had, it was,

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:50.980
we could walk up and down
this road when we was kids.

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:53.110
Nobody locked their doors.

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:55.220
Nobody, you could go in.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:58.535
We could go in eat, and if
they had anything left over,

00:07:58.535 --> 00:08:01.010
cornbread or anything.

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:03.650
Go in the house, go in
there in the old cupboard

00:08:04.697 --> 00:08:07.330
and the wood stove, get what you wanted.

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:08.320
Nobody said nothing.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.026
Nobody was never missed nothing.

00:08:10.026 --> 00:08:12.557
But now you can't walk up down here,

00:08:12.557 --> 00:08:15.157
you have to have big old
bat to beat 'em outta here.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:16.853
No hitch.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:22.800
I don't mind givin' people
stuff, don't mind a helpin' 'em.

00:08:22.964 --> 00:08:25.170
But, boys, don't steal
somethin' off of me.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:29.046
That makes me madder than (indistinct)

00:08:29.046 --> 00:08:31.400
And tear somethin' up, if
you're goin' tear somethin' up,

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:32.770
tear up your own stuff.

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.920
Don't go around tearin'
up somebody else's.

00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:38.505
That's the way I was brought up.

00:08:38.505 --> 00:08:39.880
Dad'd skin us alive

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:42.673
he'd know we'd been out
tearin' somethin' up.

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:49.020
We'd, we'd block, we
got out here Halloween,

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:50.920
you know, we was a bunch a kids.

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:53.500
My sister and Eva Jean,

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.460
and all them fellers up here next to us,

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:57.363
they was dressed up real fat.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:00.160
So, we got a wise ideal.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:01.350
Well, we went up the road

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:02.183
and they's comin' up through there.

00:09:02.183 --> 00:09:03.960
They had pillars and stuff, you know.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.798
We stretched a rope cross
road and tripped 'em all.

00:09:06.798 --> 00:09:08.020
(Mountian Man laughing)

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.044
They was about to kill us.

00:09:09.044 --> 00:09:09.896
(Mountian Man laughing)

00:09:09.896 --> 00:09:13.398
They'd yell, "If I get
ahold of ya, we'll kill ya!"

00:09:13.398 --> 00:09:15.490
And Minnie up here,

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:18.427
we went up to her door, told Minnie,

00:09:18.427 --> 00:09:21.933
"We gonna trick or treat ya!
And if you don't treat us,

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:27.692
we gonna pile you up on with set."

00:09:27.692 --> 00:09:28.946
She said, "Okay, boys, okay."

00:09:28.946 --> 00:09:29.980
Well, we did.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:34.205
She come out with a water hose
and drowneded everyone of us.

00:09:34.205 --> 00:09:35.166
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:09:35.166 --> 00:09:38.614
She said now, "That's the
treat I'm gonna give ya."

00:09:38.614 --> 00:09:42.306
She drowneded us. But,
I mean, flat drowneded.

00:09:42.306 --> 00:09:46.038
And, that's the way it's here.

00:09:46.038 --> 00:09:51.038
He'd set in with ya, old
man Taylor Brown, in school,

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:54.953
he was rough on us.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:59.893
But he was the best teacher,
I'd recommend him to anybody.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.343
We went on a field trip now,

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:08.410
up on the ship where we come
off, the Bigelow up there,

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:11.800
to learn the trees and
the flowers, you know,

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.410
the leaves, and identify 'em.

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:19.410
And, well, we had all the
classroom went up there too.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:26.510
So, we went and got, we
went and got excited.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.110
We'd hold hands with them girls.

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:31.280
Yeah, we was goin' up through
there a holdin' hands,

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:32.690
you know.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:34.843
Old Taylor Brown, he ever had a word.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:37.143
Not even a word.

00:10:38.306 --> 00:10:40.930
We got back to the classroom in the school

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:42.963
up here at Greenhill where it was at,

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:45.367
we got back the classroom,

00:10:45.367 --> 00:10:50.367
Taylor Brown come out and
he said, "Now, boys, girls."

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:54.550
He said, "This ain't pup, we
ain't in here for puppy love."

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:56.300
He said, "We in here to learn."

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.190
He wore everybody in there out.

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:00.540
He would give every one of 'em a whippin'.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:02.490
The whole classroom.

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:06.200
Yeah, and people, they respected him.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:09.650
He'd take us, when we'd
go to the first grade,

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:11.420
down the spelling bee.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:14.670
We'd had to spell, in
the first grade book,

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:18.260
we'd spell it till we missed a word.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:20.060
We'd go to the second grade book,

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.290
you'd spell till you
missed more than that.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:25.827
And everybody was competin'
to stay ahead of the other.

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:32.607
And he taught me, one thing
he taught me about smokin',

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:36.603
he said, "Tobaccer is an Indian weed,

00:11:36.603 --> 00:11:40.980
from the devil it proceeds.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:43.440
He picks your pockets, burns your clothes,

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.400
and makes a chimney out of your nose."

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:50.400
That's a, I won't never forget
what that, as long as I live.

00:11:50.545 --> 00:11:51.600
- [Mark] So you never smoked?

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:52.433
- No.

00:11:52.433 --> 00:11:54.643
- [Mark] What about
alcohol or stuff like that?

00:11:54.643 --> 00:11:56.080
- Nah, I aint never drunk none.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:59.264
I drank a beer every now and then.

00:11:59.264 --> 00:12:00.472
- [Mark] What about moonshine?

00:12:00.472 --> 00:12:02.470
- Well, you know, you sip it a little bit.

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:04.560
- [Mark] Your family made moonshine?

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.597
- Made it, I'd like to
have what they made.

00:12:07.597 --> 00:12:08.830
(Mountain Man chuckling)

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:11.120
Everything down this creek made moonshine.

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:13.400
They wasn't no house
around here that didn't.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:14.233
- [Mark] Really?

00:12:14.233 --> 00:12:17.793
- Yeah. They had, when
the revenues come in,

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:20.113
they had a signal.

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.086
Well, if they come in on this one,

00:12:24.086 --> 00:12:26.873
he'd fire his old shotgun three times.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:30.340
That means the law was in here.

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:32.740
And everybody'd leave
their still and come home.

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:36.610
And that's the way they
warned one another.

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:38.140
Dad made moonshine.

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:41.980
He would haul it to, there
was an old colored fellow

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:44.302
over there in Georgetown.

00:12:44.302 --> 00:12:49.003
He bought every gallon
of moonshine dad made.

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:53.103
Dad took the back seat of the car out.

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:57.980
He'd stack his moonshine
in there on top of it,

00:12:57.980 --> 00:12:59.707
put a quilt and stuff over it.

00:12:59.707 --> 00:13:02.230
And the kids would sit
on it in the back seat

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.620
and he'd take it across
the mountain to town.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.120
Take it over there to him.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.930
We got over there and he told us,

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:10.763
he said, "Boys,"

00:13:10.763 --> 00:13:13.147
he said, "I'd sure like
to have some good wine."

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:19.650
And dad didn't fool with
wine, he didn't make no wine.

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:21.720
So, we picked a bunch of blackberries

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:23.703
and made him up a batch of wine.

00:13:25.193 --> 00:13:28.428
He come over here and
bought every bit of it.

00:13:28.428 --> 00:13:31.170
So, we got in a big hurry,

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:32.870
we're gonna fix him another batch.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:36.640
We got in a big hurry and jarred it up

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:38.410
before it worked off,

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:41.270
put it in a closet in the back room.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:43.370
We was a sittin' in there at the fireplace

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:45.783
and somethin', pow!

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.290
Dad said, "What is that?"

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:52.097
I don't know, somethin went pow again.

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:57.227
We went in there and that
blackberry wine was a blowin' up.

00:13:57.227 --> 00:14:00.343
It busted jars, running
everywhere in that closet.

00:14:00.343 --> 00:14:01.197
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:14:01.197 --> 00:14:04.970
So, we had to, that ended
the blackberry wine makin'

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:06.061
right there.

00:14:06.061 --> 00:14:09.050
We jarred it, if you jar
it before it works off,

00:14:09.050 --> 00:14:10.200
it'll blow up.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:11.033
- [Mark] Oh, really?

00:14:11.033 --> 00:14:11.866
- Yeah.

00:14:11.866 --> 00:14:14.090
- [Mark] So, what kind of jobs
have you had in your life?

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:15.449
- I worked in the mines.

00:14:15.449 --> 00:14:20.449
Worked in the, I worked at
a boiler shop in Chicago.

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:22.509
- [Mark] So, you lived
in Chicago for a while?

00:14:22.509 --> 00:14:23.350
- Yeah.

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:24.183
- [Mark] How long?

00:14:24.183 --> 00:14:26.440
- Yeah, I was up there
probably a year or two

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:29.610
when I got laid off,
and I never did go back.

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.063
- [Mark] How'd you like
living in the big city?

00:14:31.063 --> 00:14:35.183
- Don't. It'd be all right if
you was out in the country.

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:40.163
I lived on the South side,
Tilton High School there.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:44.023
And there was fightin' every
night and all day long.

00:14:45.561 --> 00:14:50.561
So, we got, we got, we
got, we get up in a day,

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:57.440
and I said, "If I ever get
laid off, never comin' back."

00:14:57.440 --> 00:14:59.917
And I ain't been back
since I got laid off.

00:14:59.917 --> 00:15:02.550
- [Mark] And you love it
here? You love it here?

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:03.670
- Yeah, man.

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:05.600
This is the only place to be.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.218
- [Mark] What do you
love about Appalachia?

00:15:07.218 --> 00:15:10.037
- It's better, well, look at it.

00:15:10.037 --> 00:15:11.700
You can see it.

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:12.570
- [Mark] It's beautiful, yeah.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:13.403
- Yeah.

00:15:13.403 --> 00:15:14.236
- [Mark] But, it's very poor here.

00:15:14.236 --> 00:15:15.880
- They ain't nobody,

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:18.313
Most of your neighbors are just like me.

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:21.780
They good neighbors.

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:23.650
You've talked to 'em and you go visit 'em.

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:26.023
They want to just to be left alone.

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:29.530
Don't go up there and
steal this or steal that.

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:32.378
Go up there and ask 'em,
and nine times out of 10,

00:15:32.378 --> 00:15:34.373
they'd give it to ya.

00:15:34.373 --> 00:15:36.660
That is what it is here.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:38.510
Just this junk a sittin' round here.

00:15:38.510 --> 00:15:42.530
If you need it and I own
it, I'll give it to ya.

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:45.630
But don't come in here and
try to steal it and take it.

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:46.930
I mean that ain't no good.

00:15:47.793 --> 00:15:48.767
Dad'd set us a fire if he
know'd we stole somethin'.

00:15:51.758 --> 00:15:54.650
We went to old man, at brother of mine,

00:15:54.650 --> 00:15:58.080
went to old man Anderson Buckheart,

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:00.203
on top of Pine Mountain here, had a store.

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:02.713
That's where dad'd trade.

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:09.070
We'd go up there when we was kids,

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:11.043
and the first thing we'd do,

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:13.500
had big cracks in the floor

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:15.583
where'd he sit with his cash register.

00:16:16.446 --> 00:16:18.393
And he had them,

00:16:21.384 --> 00:16:23.660
we'd go around the back
and dive under that floor

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:24.610
and look for money.

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:29.150
And, every time we'd go
in there under that floor,

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:32.330
we'd find enough money to
buy us a pop and a Moonpie.

00:16:32.330 --> 00:16:35.542
I think he was a feedin' it
down through the floor to us,

00:16:35.542 --> 00:16:37.350
what he was a doin'.

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:39.260
Every time we went up there.

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:40.330
That brother of mine,

00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:41.703
Junior, he got up,

00:16:42.842 --> 00:16:45.533
he decided he'd steal
him a pack of cigarettes.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:51.580
Dad, he stole that pack of cigarettes.

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:54.707
Dad caught him with 'em.

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:58.878
Now, he said, "Son, you're
goin' back up there,

00:16:58.878 --> 00:17:01.660
you gonna pay the old
man for the cigarette.

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:04.743
And he said, "You're gonna
wish I hadn't set ya on fire."

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:07.797
He come back up, old man Buckheart said,

00:17:07.797 --> 00:17:08.840
"I seen him get 'em."

00:17:08.840 --> 00:17:10.830
He said, "I wasn't gonna say nothin'."

00:17:10.830 --> 00:17:13.340
He said, "'Cause I know
that if Jim bought 'em,

00:17:13.340 --> 00:17:15.910
found 'em, he'd wear ya
out and bring 'em back."

00:17:15.910 --> 00:17:18.870
He made, and he'd go up there
and tell the man he was sorry,

00:17:18.870 --> 00:17:20.692
and pay him for his cigarette.

00:17:20.692 --> 00:17:23.498
Wouldn't let him have 'em.

00:17:23.498 --> 00:17:27.790
That's the way we was raised
up, to respect your elder.

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:31.839
I mean, they got no respect
for elder people no more.

00:17:31.839 --> 00:17:35.390
People raised up now,
it's, they don't care.

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:36.223
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:17:36.223 --> 00:17:37.640
What do you think of the
younger generation here?

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:40.140
A lot of drugs are being used?

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:41.470
- [Mountain Man] Well, a lot of it, yeah.

00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:46.470
I mean, they just ain't, they
need to get out any of 'em.

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:50.100
I did it. Everybody else did it.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:51.260
I think he,

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:55.193
all these young generations
need to serve in the Service.

00:17:55.193 --> 00:17:58.093
I think the draft needs
to be back in his country.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:03.680
'Cause I was an 18, couldn't find no work,

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:09.510
and I went to place the
draft place down there.

00:18:09.510 --> 00:18:13.090
I said, "Boys, don't be
sending me a bunch of papers

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:14.196
like this."

00:18:14.196 --> 00:18:15.868
I said, "Just go ahead and draft me."

00:18:15.868 --> 00:18:17.055
Well, I didn't hear from 'em

00:18:17.055 --> 00:18:18.053
and I got me a good job a working.

00:18:18.961 --> 00:18:20.534
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:18:20.534 --> 00:18:22.522
Two weeks after I got
the job, they drafted me.

00:18:22.522 --> 00:18:23.510
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:24.343
- [Mark] Wow.

00:18:24.343 --> 00:18:26.560
- [Mountain Man] So, there you go.

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:28.110
- [Mark] So, Mountain Man,

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:29.700
do you consider yourself a hillbilly?

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:30.533
- Huh?

00:18:30.533 --> 00:18:32.629
- [Mark] Do you consider
yourself a hillbilly?

00:18:32.629 --> 00:18:34.982
- [Mountain Man] No, I
just, it's a hillbilly over.

00:18:34.982 --> 00:18:36.180
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:38.430
That's what I think, right here.

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:39.500
You can't give me none of that,

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:42.450
bib overalls are the only thing I wear.

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:43.417
- [Mark] Yeah. And what do you,

00:18:43.417 --> 00:18:46.547
how do you define, what
is a hillbilly to you?

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:49.470
- [Mountain Man] Plain old country fella

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:51.093
like my dad and mom was.

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:56.963
They would help, we'd
walk, grandma from here,

00:18:58.017 --> 00:19:01.188
she'd ride a mule into
Middleport, stay all night,

00:19:01.188 --> 00:19:06.070
ride that mule on to Hyden
to get some groceries,

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:09.407
and then mule back to
Middleport and stay all night,

00:19:09.407 --> 00:19:11.333
and come on home the next day.

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:16.827
And, transportation, it was here,

00:19:16.827 --> 00:19:18.740
but this but this was a gravel road.

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:21.727
Nothin' but gravel on it.

00:19:21.727 --> 00:19:23.330
You didn't have, didn't have,

00:19:23.330 --> 00:19:26.340
I think they pitched
that in '53, or '56, one.

00:19:29.476 --> 00:19:30.893
- [Mark] When did you start
seeing automobiles here?

00:19:30.893 --> 00:19:31.726
- Huh?

00:19:31.726 --> 00:19:33.980
- [Mark] When did you start
seeing automobiles here?

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:38.320
- That, we had an automobile,
that old cousin of mine did.

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:40.420
I'll tell you a good one we pulled on him.

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:45.540
He got, we got up on
it, he had an old '55,

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:48.892
a '52, I believe it
was, a Chevrolet, a '49.

00:19:48.892 --> 00:19:49.725
- [Mark] How old are you?

00:19:49.725 --> 00:19:50.558
- Huh?

00:19:50.558 --> 00:19:51.580
- [Mark] How old are you?

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:53.100
- I'll be, I'm 78.

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:55.550
- [Mark] Seventy-eight.

00:19:55.550 --> 00:19:58.367
- We had some firecrackers in our hand.

00:19:58.367 --> 00:19:59.880
We got on the running board,

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:01.889
and I guess he thought

00:20:01.889 --> 00:20:04.220
if we was ridin' down the road with him,

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:06.119
I guess he thought we was supposed to

00:20:06.119 --> 00:20:07.441
light them firecrackers.

00:20:07.441 --> 00:20:10.682
He lit the firecrackers
that we had in our hand.

00:20:10.682 --> 00:20:14.756
You talkin' 'bout burn.
Boy, were we burn up.

00:20:14.756 --> 00:20:18.849
And he got him, we got him
to pull, couldn't, you know,

00:20:18.849 --> 00:20:22.664
it would salt this road
when it froze over.

00:20:22.664 --> 00:20:26.997
We got him to pull us on
a, on a one of them sleds.

00:20:29.620 --> 00:20:31.730
Well, he was pullin' us down
the road and he stopped.

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:33.120
We kept goin', we couldn't stop,

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:34.415
right up and under the truck.

00:20:34.415 --> 00:20:36.201
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:20:36.201 --> 00:20:38.530
Came up from behind him on a rope.

00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:41.240
And we got, my uncle up here,

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:43.554
we made us a road down
the hill like this here.

00:20:43.554 --> 00:20:47.100
Well, we didn't have no sled's.

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:50.220
We got all of his hickory bottom chairs.

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:52.910
We was up there, had one
of them, they'd fly me.

00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:55.260
But when it hit bottom,
it'd bust all to pieces.

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:57.670
There was no chair left.

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:59.193
He about to killed us.

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:02.980
And that old school house that
we first went to school in

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:08.600
was up here above me here.

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:09.713
Two room school.

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:15.160
And we got out, my uncle,
he couldn't get backed in.

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:17.430
He couldn't get no sugar mud.

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:19.630
Well, they made the
moonshine with molasses.

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:23.383
We got out there and
got in his smokehouse,

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:27.200
and what we didn't just
open one can of molasses.

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:28.850
Everybody's got a can,

00:21:28.850 --> 00:21:31.037
had their fingers in there just eatin'.

00:21:31.037 --> 00:21:34.157
Buddy, he went plum off
of it when he caught us.

00:21:34.157 --> 00:21:36.730
He said, "You're destroyin' me."

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:38.910
And he said, "You're wastin' it."

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:41.780
He said, "It wouldn't be so
bad if you just got one."

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:44.640
He said, "Every one of you got one open."

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:47.973
But I can take you up here on the hill,

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:51.680
and I can find them old
molasses cans up there

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:54.337
where they made moonshine
all over the place.

00:21:55.427 --> 00:22:00.427
But that moonshine ain't
that hard to make, no how.

00:22:01.790 --> 00:22:04.383
But, you got to know what
you're doin' to make it.

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:10.360
'Cause if you scorch it, if
you puke it, or any of that,

00:22:11.086 --> 00:22:13.423
Oh, Lord, you can't drink it.

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:15.650
So, you're in trouble.

00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:17.790
- [Mark] So, have you, have you
traveled around the country?

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:18.623
- No.

00:22:18.623 --> 00:22:23.063
The only places I've been
when I was in the service.

00:22:23.063 --> 00:22:23.923
- [Mark] You went to Chicago.

00:22:23.923 --> 00:22:27.254
- I've went, yeah, I've been to Chicago.

00:22:27.254 --> 00:22:29.233
I've been down in Florida.

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:34.802
Been down up in, uh, I ain't
been nowhere, hardly. Mud.

00:22:34.802 --> 00:22:35.635
- [Mark] North Carolina.

00:22:35.635 --> 00:22:36.554
- North Carolina.

00:22:36.554 --> 00:22:39.872
Yeah, I was down in
North Carolina one time.

00:22:39.872 --> 00:22:44.250
And I went to, I took my Basic Training

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:46.223
in Fort Jackson, South Carolina.

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:50.293
Took my AIT in Fort Leonardwood, Missouri.

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:53.563
Come back to Fort Eustis, Virginia.

00:22:53.563 --> 00:22:56.563
I was in Fort Eustis in the shipyard

00:22:59.524 --> 00:23:02.274
when Kennedy got killed up there.

00:23:04.032 --> 00:23:06.449
And, that is the most of the,

00:23:07.613 --> 00:23:09.912
when they told me, I was,

00:23:09.912 --> 00:23:11.040
I got out of Basic Training,

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:13.410
they was gonna send me to a Little Korea.

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:14.666
I didn't know what they
were talkin' about.

00:23:14.666 --> 00:23:16.724
I said, "Ya ain't sendin' me over there."

00:23:16.724 --> 00:23:21.340
And it went to Fort Leonardwood,
Missouri, is what it was.

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:23.530
You had only, you didn't
have, they's nothing.

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:26.873
It used to be an old German
prison camp, what it was.

00:23:27.896 --> 00:23:30.900
So, they, you didn't have nothin'

00:23:30.900 --> 00:23:32.650
but one little town of Waynesville.

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:37.332
And all rest of it, if
you got a weekend pass,

00:23:37.332 --> 00:23:38.970
was 100 and some miles away.

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:40.670
You couldn't do that in a weekend.

00:23:41.822 --> 00:23:44.040
So, I could go downtown Waynesville,

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:45.970
and it was about the size of Harlan,

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:48.297
wasn't as big as Harlan.
That's all it was.

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:51.640
I stayed on base.

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:53.903
You could go to a movie for a quarter,

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:57.150
and they had five theaters on there.

00:23:57.150 --> 00:23:58.984
So, I'd go to all the theaters

00:23:58.984 --> 00:24:00.634
while there was different movies.

00:24:02.543 --> 00:24:05.294
It used to be a German
prison camp, what it was.

00:24:05.294 --> 00:24:06.127
- [Mark] Oh, is that right?

00:24:06.127 --> 00:24:07.440
- Fort Leonardwood, yeah.

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:10.050
They had the old machine
gun mounts and stuff,

00:24:10.050 --> 00:24:11.140
everything was still there

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:13.932
where they kept German prisoners there.

00:24:13.932 --> 00:24:14.765
- [Mark] Wow.

00:24:14.765 --> 00:24:18.890
- Yeah, that's where we built,
built bridges, tore it down.

00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:20.277
Build a bridge, tear it down.

00:24:20.277 --> 00:24:24.150
Build it, tear it down.
Combat Engineers. Yeah.

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:26.057
- [Mark] So has life here gotten easier?

00:24:26.057 --> 00:24:27.113
Gotten harder?

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:31.867
- No, it ain't got no, no harder for me,

00:24:31.867 --> 00:24:33.263
if you know how to it.

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:37.510
I mean, I got, I don't pay no water bill.

00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:39.793
I get my mountain up here with my water.

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:43.223
And my electricity ain't that much.

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:49.150
I got, I draw Social Security,

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:51.507
but it don't draw enough to pay the bills.

00:24:52.361 --> 00:24:55.270
That's the reason I have to,
on the side, and you know,

00:24:55.270 --> 00:24:56.950
I sell a little junk and stuff.

00:24:56.950 --> 00:24:58.480
Keep my spendin' money.

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:00.048
- [Mark] And do you hunt for food?

00:25:00.048 --> 00:25:02.250
- Oh, yeah, they plenty of...

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:04.480
oh, I could feed you all you want.

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:05.313
- [Mark] What kind of stuff do you...?

00:25:05.313 --> 00:25:08.220
- There's plenty a elk
right here, deer too.

00:25:08.220 --> 00:25:10.780
Turkeys. All that.

00:25:10.780 --> 00:25:14.110
In here, you can survive
in these mountains

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:15.310
if somethin' goes wrong.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:17.606
In these cities, you can't.

00:25:17.606 --> 00:25:18.773
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:22.330
- It's survival out here you can do.

00:25:22.330 --> 00:25:25.040
And in the city, you can't
do what you can do here.

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:26.650
- [Mark] Is that what makes
the Appalachian people

00:25:26.650 --> 00:25:29.465
so unique, and so strong, and so good?

00:25:29.465 --> 00:25:30.440
- Yeah, I mean, they survive.

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:31.273
- [Mark] They're survivors here?

00:25:31.273 --> 00:25:34.420
- Yeah. Well, you can do
anything like that, you know.

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:37.570
You can go up here, if I'm hungry,

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:39.420
I'll go over here and kill me a deer.

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:42.437
If I get hungry, I'm gonna feed my family,

00:25:42.437 --> 00:25:43.563
one way or the other,

00:25:43.563 --> 00:25:46.608
whether I got a license or not.

00:25:46.608 --> 00:25:48.694
But if you go into the city, you can't,

00:25:48.694 --> 00:25:51.610
you probably gotta kill one
of them big rats and eat it.

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:53.330
But, who would do that?

00:25:54.435 --> 00:25:56.793
So, this is a survivor place, basically.

00:25:56.793 --> 00:25:58.357
- [Mark] - Yeah, so, I like....

00:25:58.357 --> 00:25:59.190
- Anybody can.

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:01.946
- [Mark] I see your hat.
You voted for Trump?

00:26:01.946 --> 00:26:05.168
- Voted for Trump, support Trump 100%.

00:26:05.168 --> 00:26:07.038
- [Mark] What do you like
best about what he's done?

00:26:07.038 --> 00:26:07.871
- Huh?

00:26:07.871 --> 00:26:09.370
- [Mark] What do you
like best about Trump?

00:26:09.370 --> 00:26:12.943
- Buddy, he's done for the
Veterans that nobody'd done.

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:18.010
We can go anywhere we
want to see a doctor.

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:22.843
Now, he done what he
said he was going to do,

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:25.570
he done whatever he said.

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:29.704
And I can't see these
fellers, you know, out here,

00:26:29.704 --> 00:26:32.600
tearing up somethin', burnin' it down.

00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:35.958
Why don't they go home and
burn their own house down?

00:26:35.958 --> 00:26:40.958
You know? It, things are wrong,
but there's a way to do it,

00:26:42.910 --> 00:26:43.923
and a wrong way.

00:26:45.528 --> 00:26:47.560
Now, if I got somethin' against you,

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:49.833
and I can't go to court and settle it,

00:26:51.100 --> 00:26:54.343
well, they'll get out here
and go burn your house down.

00:26:54.343 --> 00:26:55.990
It's ridiculous.

00:26:56.897 --> 00:26:58.240
But I voted for him.

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:00.258
I'll vote for him again.

00:27:00.258 --> 00:27:03.090
- [Mark] Did you have, do you
have any regrets in your life?

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:03.943
- No, not really.

00:27:05.270 --> 00:27:09.920
I had my wife here for 50
years and she passed away.

00:27:09.920 --> 00:27:12.910
You know, I wish she could still be here.

00:27:12.910 --> 00:27:14.820
- [Mark] And how many kids do you have?

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:15.653
- Huh?

00:27:15.653 --> 00:27:16.486
- [Mark] How many kids do you have?

00:27:16.486 --> 00:27:19.297
- I got, let's see, I'd
have to count 'em now.

00:27:20.623 --> 00:27:22.991
I believe they was seven of 'em.

00:27:22.991 --> 00:27:24.010
- [Mark] And you raised them?

00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:25.300
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:26.225
- [Mark] They're good kids?

00:27:26.225 --> 00:27:27.680
- Every one of 'em.

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:28.513
- [Mark] That's great.

00:27:28.513 --> 00:27:32.650
- Hey, you can't, you
can raise, you can't let,

00:27:32.650 --> 00:27:34.651
you gotta have this one
one over here to raise.

00:27:34.651 --> 00:27:36.734
If you ain't gonna have
kids and raise 'em,

00:27:36.734 --> 00:27:39.163
why would I have 'em. You know?

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:44.450
But, when I was a kid,

00:27:44.450 --> 00:27:48.093
we worked up here at
the big old coal line,

00:27:49.111 --> 00:27:50.150
and, we'd work an hour

00:27:50.150 --> 00:27:53.860
just so we could go play the
pinball machine. You know?

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:55.160
We'd work in there.

00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:57.840
He said, "Now, boys,"
he said, "if you fellers

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:00.560
do a man's work, I'll
pay you a man's pay."

00:28:01.951 --> 00:28:05.243
We'd go up there and work,
and he pay up 50 cents hour.

00:28:05.243 --> 00:28:07.013
That's what we got.

00:28:07.013 --> 00:28:08.680
And we'd go up there

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:11.550
and we'd put it back in to Pat's store,

00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:14.529
in the pinball machine.
That's what we done.

00:28:14.529 --> 00:28:16.129
But, you can buy, you could buy,

00:28:17.746 --> 00:28:19.693
he had 'em on sale,

00:28:20.590 --> 00:28:24.663
a 10 pound block candy at Christmas.

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:27.277
Me and that cousin, I told him, I said,

00:28:27.277 --> 00:28:29.093
"Let's buy one of them."

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:32.080
We split on it.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:33.830
I think it was a dollar and a half.

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:36.737
And I told him, I said,

00:28:36.737 --> 00:28:39.507
"Now, we gonna have to eat
this thing for we get home."

00:28:41.388 --> 00:28:42.910
'Cause if you don't,

00:28:42.910 --> 00:28:45.748
the big fellers would
take it away from us.

00:28:45.748 --> 00:28:47.620
Me and him eat that 10 pound of candy.

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:48.970
We never eat no more candy.

00:28:49.930 --> 00:28:51.130
We was sicker 'an mules.

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:54.540
I think it's gonna get wet.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:55.590
- [Mark] Yeah. I think
it's startin' to rain.

00:28:55.590 --> 00:28:56.479
- Yep.

00:28:56.479 --> 00:28:58.000
- [Mark] What's the most important lesson

00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.158
you've learned in your life?

00:28:59.158 --> 00:29:00.070
- Huh?

00:29:00.070 --> 00:29:01.290
- [Mark] What's the most important lesson

00:29:01.290 --> 00:29:03.770
you've learned in your
life, in your 78 years?

00:29:03.770 --> 00:29:08.770
- Well, to do what others, really, I mean,

00:29:08.866 --> 00:29:10.900
the Bible says, "Do unto others,

00:29:10.900 --> 00:29:12.600
what you'd have them do un..."

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:16.300
I like to be treated just
like I want to treat somebody,

00:29:16.300 --> 00:29:18.930
I want to be treated the same way.

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:23.523
And it, you, you can do
anything you wanna do.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:26.427
And someday, it's gonna come back on you,

00:29:26.427 --> 00:29:27.513
and you gonna regret.

00:29:29.159 --> 00:29:33.520
So, I like to be treated
just like everybody else.

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:37.810
And, I don't care what color
you are, or how you are.

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:42.810
See, grandma was part Indian,

00:29:44.522 --> 00:29:48.029
other words, there's Indian in all of us.

00:29:48.029 --> 00:29:49.923
She was a part Cherokee Indian.

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:54.300
And years ago,

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:58.620
you can't see people tearing
down all the statutes,

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:00.190
all the history.

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:03.700
And especially this here, this is history.

00:30:03.700 --> 00:30:07.330
And tearing it down and destroying it,

00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:11.100
teach it, what it was,
and why it happened.

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:11.933
You know?

00:30:11.933 --> 00:30:13.853
I mean, it's simple.

00:30:14.930 --> 00:30:17.220
But they want to tear it down, destroy it.

00:30:17.220 --> 00:30:20.281
And stuff at you can't put back.

00:30:20.281 --> 00:30:23.253
They tore the old Greenhills School down.

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:26.270
They shouldn't have never done that.

00:30:26.270 --> 00:30:28.320
They shoulda left that there for history.

00:30:29.632 --> 00:30:31.430
And the old Keywood,
down in to Keywood here,

00:30:31.430 --> 00:30:36.250
there was a CCC Camp, they tore it down.

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:40.330
And history, people woulda come
in here by oodlums to visit

00:30:40.330 --> 00:30:41.753
and do stuff in that.

00:30:43.220 --> 00:30:46.630
So, it's, that's history.

00:30:46.630 --> 00:30:47.963
And, you know,

00:30:49.020 --> 00:30:53.437
I don't regret nothin' that I've done.

00:30:53.437 --> 00:30:55.110
- [Mark] are you a religious man?

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:55.943
- Huh?

00:30:55.943 --> 00:30:57.640
- [Mark] Are you religious?

00:30:57.640 --> 00:30:58.980
- Well, if you ain't,

00:30:58.980 --> 00:30:59.813
I believe in the Bible

00:30:59.813 --> 00:31:02.630
that ain't never would
be any joining no church.

00:31:02.630 --> 00:31:03.463
But, if I believe

00:31:03.463 --> 00:31:07.765
that if a man does exactly
what that Bible says,

00:31:07.765 --> 00:31:09.440
he's going to Heaven.

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:10.590
I don't care who it is.

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:13.643
- [Mark] All right, Mountain Man,

00:31:13.643 --> 00:31:14.790
thank you so much for talking with me.

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:15.623
- Yeah.

00:31:15.623 --> 00:31:16.456
- [Mark] Very interesting story.

00:31:16.456 --> 00:31:17.289
- Yeah.

00:31:17.289 --> 00:31:19.287
- [Mark] And good luck with
whatever you do from here.

00:31:19.287 --> 00:31:21.270
- Yeah, well, this is country here.

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:22.130
- [Mark] Yeah. You're happy here.

00:31:22.130 --> 00:31:22.963
- Yeah.

00:31:22.963 --> 00:31:23.910
- [Mark] It's beautiful.

00:31:23.910 --> 00:31:25.940
- [Mountain Man] I'd rather
be sittin' on a tin can

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:29.789
than on a dag burn rocking
chair and have to pay for it.

00:31:29.789 --> 00:31:31.293
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:31:31.293 --> 00:31:33.680
You know? You can make anything work.

00:31:35.071 --> 00:31:36.430
The only way to go.

00:31:36.430 --> 00:31:37.564
- [Mark] Excellent.

00:31:37.564 --> 00:31:39.020
Mountain Man, thank you so much.

00:31:39.020 --> 00:31:40.971
- Yeah. Well, you're welcome.

00:31:40.971 --> 00:31:43.040
I mean, you can come
around anytime you want to.

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:43.921
- [Mark] I'll be back.

00:31:43.921 --> 00:31:45.660
- And if ya'll want to
come down and stay a week,

00:31:45.660 --> 00:31:47.250
we'll run you all over this country.

00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:50.062
(both laughing)

00:31:50.062 --> 00:31:50.895
- [Mark] How many generations

00:31:50.895 --> 00:31:52.813
do you think this property's
been in your family?

00:31:54.070 --> 00:31:57.743
- Well, grandma was 100,
almost 102 when she died.

00:31:59.025 --> 00:32:00.883
So it's been here ever since.

00:32:00.883 --> 00:32:01.883
- [Mark] Oh.

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:05.913
- They used to own, grandpa and them did,

00:32:06.966 --> 00:32:11.030
own from the head of the creek,
all this back through here,

00:32:11.030 --> 00:32:12.720
they used to own.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:14.840
Now, they sold this to
Ashland Land Company

00:32:14.840 --> 00:32:16.063
and went to Oklahoma.

00:32:17.795 --> 00:32:20.410
Then then, out there, then they packed up

00:32:20.410 --> 00:32:21.527
and come back here.

00:32:21.527 --> 00:32:25.090
And they had saved this, if
they ever wanted to come back.

00:32:25.090 --> 00:32:26.943
And they come back here.

00:32:28.630 --> 00:32:30.710
They went and bought a big
farm there in Oklahoma.

00:32:30.710 --> 00:32:31.750
- [Mark] You're not leaving?

00:32:31.750 --> 00:32:32.583
- Huh? No.

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:34.839
I'm not goin' nowhere.

00:32:34.839 --> 00:32:36.210
(Mountain Man laughing)

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:39.700
I wouldn't give this for
all the cities in the world.

00:32:39.700 --> 00:32:40.533
- [Mark] It's beautiful here.

00:32:40.533 --> 00:32:43.070
- Yeah. Well, you got plenty.

00:32:43.070 --> 00:32:44.947
I can get my water back yonder.

00:32:48.320 --> 00:32:52.671
It's about 1,000 feet up yonder,

00:32:52.671 --> 00:32:54.483
where dad and 'em made moonshine.

00:32:55.920 --> 00:33:00.920
Yeah. That's where ya, there's
people in Chicago and places,

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:05.503
that would give their neck to get this.

